{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-162791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=-4x+y+4\\,$ on the parabola $8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4=8\\lambda  \\\\ 1=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2(-\\frac{1}{2})}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expression for $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-{y}^{2}=1 \\\\ 8x-1 & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{4},1).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens to the right, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{4},1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ and its maximum value is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-162791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4x+16y\\,$ on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,16⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4=2\\lambda x \\\\ 16=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{4+16}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ | $\\,8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its minimum value is $-8\\sqrt{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-99039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-16\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "-20\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "-40\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "-8\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=-2x+y-2\\,$ on the parabola ${x}^{2}-y=1.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-y=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ \\lambda =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & =1 \\\\ 1-y & =1 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(1,0).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens upwards, there isn't a maximum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the minimum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(1,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(1,0),$ and its minimum value is $-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-162786" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162786" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162786" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=3x-y+6\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=10\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=10$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 3,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-3,1),\\,(3,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-3,1)\\,$ | $\\,(3,-1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,16\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(3,-1),$ and its maximum value is $16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-99030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=1-x-y\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-1=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,1+\\sqrt{2}\\,$ | $\\,1-\\sqrt{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),$ and its maximum value is $1+\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-99027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "1+\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "1-\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=3.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the hyperbola.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2=-2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}1-x\\lambda =0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-3 & =0\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+x(-1)=0\\,⇒\\,x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,-2),(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-2),\\,(1,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(1,-2)$ | $(1,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(1,\\pm 2)$ and its maximum value is $-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-162769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2\\,$ on the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=8\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(1-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-8 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2\\sqrt{2}),(0,2\\sqrt{2}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1-\\frac{1}{4})=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\sqrt{2},0),(\\sqrt{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0),\\,(0,\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(0,\\pm 2\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and its minimum value is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-162760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1-2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-2x=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}2x(1+2\\lambda )=1 \\\\ y(1-4\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,0),(1,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1+\\frac{1}{2})=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}),(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(-1,0)$ and its minimum value is $-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-162768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "-9"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\,$ on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y+4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+4=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda -1)=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-1),(0,1).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2(1)-1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-1),\\,(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-1)$ | $(0,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,1)$ and its maximum value is $9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-162762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,$ on the parabola ${y}^{2}-4x=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={y}^{2}-4x=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-4,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=-4\\lambda  \\\\ -4y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-2\\lambda  \\\\ y(\\lambda +2)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x & =0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-2(-2)\\,⇒\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-16 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(4,-4),(4,4).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,\\pm 4).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,\\pm 4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(4,\\pm 4)$ and its minimum value is $-16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-162770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-16"] ["math" "-20"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y+4z$ on the ellipsoid $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=21.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=21.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=21\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=8\\lambda x \\\\ 1=2\\lambda y \\\\ 4=8\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{8\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=21$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =21 \\\\ 4(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}})+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4(\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =21 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =21 \\\\ \\frac{21}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =21 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,y=\\pm 2,\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},2,2),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{2},2,2)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-\\frac{21}{2}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{21}{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},-2,-2),$ and its minimum value is $-\\frac{21}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-162767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-6"] ["math" "-7"] ["math" "-9"] ["math" "-\\frac{14}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{21}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{21}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-yz$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-z,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -z=2\\lambda y \\\\ -y=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda -1)=0 \\\\ y(4{\\lambda }^{2}-1)=0 \\\\ z=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 1,0,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=0$ and $z=∓y.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+(-y)^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding values of $z$ are\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 1,0,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,∓\\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(\\pm 1,0,0),$  and its maximum value is $1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-99168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the ellipsoid $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨18x,8y,2z⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨9x,4y,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=9\\lambda x \\\\ y=4\\lambda y \\\\ z=\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}(1-9\\lambda )x=0 \\\\ (1-4\\lambda )y=0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{9},$ $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\lambda \\ne 1.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{9}.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0,0).$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n4{y}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 3,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 6.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 6).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 2,0,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\pm 3,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 6)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ | $\\,36\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 2,0,0),$ and its minimum value is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-162773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162766" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+y+5z$ on the ellipsoid $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}=38.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162766" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}=38.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=38\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,1,5⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨18x,2y,18z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=18\\lambda x \\\\ 1=2\\lambda y \\\\ 5=18\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{9\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{18\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=38$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2} & =38 \\\\ 9(\\frac{1}{81{\\lambda }^{2}})+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+9(\\frac{25}{324{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =38 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{36{\\lambda }^{2}} & =38 \\\\ \\frac{19}{18{\\lambda }^{2}} & =38 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,y=\\pm 3,\\,z=\\pm \\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(\\frac{2}{3},3,\\frac{5}{3}),\\,(-\\frac{2}{3},-3,-\\frac{5}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{2}{3},-3,-\\frac{5}{3})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{2}{3},3,\\frac{5}{3})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-\\frac{38}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{38}{3}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{2}{3},3,\\frac{5}{3}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{38}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162766" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-162766" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162766" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{25}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{38}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{41}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-162766" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{38}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+6y+10z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=35.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,6,10⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 6=2\\lambda y \\\\ 10=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=35$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{35}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1\\,y=\\pm 3\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,-3,-5),\\,(1,3,5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,-3,-5)\\,$ | $\\,(1,3,5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-70\\,$ | $\\,70\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,3,5),$ and its maximum value is $70.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3f507f856d711f9cb18debdf1d8541f837cc024e9d0bf4a55a4c8d67d9f85e2"} {"question" "q-99171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "30"] ["math" "34"] ["math" "66"] ["math" "70"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "q-99171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "70"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/state.json" "file_sha256" "78bf68f20240b9860e3b7623d30892bf465b05250e3eabcdfc3b84e09278a61b"} {"question" "e-8689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x+y\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 + 2y^2 =6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/example-8689.json" "file_sha256" "189f0bbe440429a56ed7f1267e44dd3cfbe4dc9b1bfd5d9c5ccdb353bafd61af"} {"question" "e-8689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + 2y^2 = 6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda\\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{4\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y) = 6$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{2\\lambda })}^{2}+2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4\\lambda })}^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{8{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2+1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx= \\pm 2 \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, -1 \\right), \\quad \\left(2, 1 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(-2, -1\\right) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(2, 1 \\right) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y) \\,$ | $\\, -3 \\,$ | $\\, 3 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $\\left(2, 1 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/example-8689.json" "file_sha256" "189f0bbe440429a56ed7f1267e44dd3cfbe4dc9b1bfd5d9c5ccdb353bafd61af"} {"question" "e-12456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 +4y^2-4=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/example-12456.json" "file_sha256" "dc9498010a525a4e9626fe9548e3564dd9754238b62e983ed57d80a34411686a"} {"question" "e-12456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 +4y^2 -4=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4\\lambda -1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,1 \\right), \\left(0,-1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4(1)-1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(-2,0 \\right), \\left(2,0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0, \\pm 1), \\qquad (\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 2, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 1),$ and its minimum value is $1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/example-12456.json" "file_sha256" "dc9498010a525a4e9626fe9548e3564dd9754238b62e983ed57d80a34411686a"} {"question" "e-8736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z) = x+y+z$ on the ellipsoid $x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/example-8736.json" "file_sha256" "390df978ca9aad5d2764de111c002db213fad10c6dfd47f7d1df7a2d52e7f99f"} {"question" "e-8736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = 36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/assets/0bae179a1a44fa0e659681ea8da4f648bc72b944ef6e2730079474dde5f141c3.png)\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\, y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z) = 36$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\, y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 4 \\qquad y = \\pm 4 \\qquad z= \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4, -4, -1),  \\qquad (4, 4, 1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, (-4, -4, -1) \\,$ | $\\, (4, 4, 1)  \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, -9 \\,$ | $\\, 9 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4, 4, 1),$ and its maximum value is $9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070701/example-8736.json" "file_sha256" "390df978ca9aad5d2764de111c002db213fad10c6dfd47f7d1df7a2d52e7f99f"}] "usage" {"e-12456" [] "e-8736" [] "e-8689" [] "q-162762" []} "basis" 2502 "retractions" [[17592186124735 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} + (y + 2)^{2}$ on the ellipse $x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 2 = 0$."] [17592186124339 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x+y\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 + 2y^2 =6$."] [17592186124339 :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint\n\n$$\ng(x,y)= x^2 + 2y^2 = 6\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= λ∇g(x, y) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y), g_{y}(x, y)⟩ \\\\\n⟨1, 1⟩ &= λ⟨2x, 4y⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming\n\n$$\n\\lambda\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n{1 = 2λx; 1 = 4λy⇒{x = \\frac{1}{2λ}; y = \\frac{1}{4λ}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y) = 6$ and solve for $\\lambda$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{2λ})^{2} + 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{4λ})^{2} &= 6 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{16λ^{2}} &= 6 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{8λ^{2}} &= 6 \\\\\n\\frac{2}{8λ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{8λ^{2}} &= 6 \\\\\n\\frac{2 + 1}{8λ^{2}} &= 6 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= \\frac{1}{16} \\\\\nλ &= ± \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y$. This gives\n\n$$\nx= \\pm 2 \\qquad y = \\pm 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, -1 \\right), \\quad \\left(2, 1 \\right)\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(-2, -1\\right) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(2, 1 \\right) \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, f(x,y) \\,$ | $\\, -3 \\,$ | $\\, 3 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(2, 1 \\right)\n$$\n\nand its maximum value is $3$."] [17592186124126 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 +4y^2-4=0$."] [17592186124126 :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint\n\n$$\ng(x,y)= x^2 +4y^2 -4=0\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= λ∇g(x, y) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y), g_{y}(x, y)⟩ \\\\\n⟨2x, 2y⟩ &= λ⟨2x, 8y⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{2x = 2λx; 2y = 8λy⇒{x(1 - λ) = 0; y(4λ - 1) = 0\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4 =0$ implies\n$4y^{2} - 4|= 0⇒y = ± 1$.\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,1 \\right), \\left(0,-1 \\right)$.\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n$y(4(1) - 1) = 0⇒y = 0$.\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4 =0$ implies\n$x^{2} - 4|= 0⇒x = ± 2$.\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(-2,0 \\right), \\left(2,0 \\right)$.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n$(0, \\pm 1), \\qquad (\\pm 2,0)$.\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 2, 0)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 1)$, and its minimum value is $1$."] [17592186124342 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z) = x+y+z$ on the ellipsoid $x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36$."] [17592186124342 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = 36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= λ∇g(x, y, z) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y, z), f_{y}(x, y, z), f_{z}(x, y, z)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y, z), g_{y}(x, y, z), g_{z}(x, y, z)⟩ \\\\\n⟨1, 1, 1⟩ &= λ⟨2x, 2y, 8z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming\n\n$$\n\\lambda \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n{1 = 2λx; 1 = 2λy; 1 = 8λz\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\, y$ and $z$ into the condition\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z) = 36\n$$\n\nand solve for $\\lambda$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 4z^{2} &= 36 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + 4(\\frac{1}{64λ^{2}}) &= 36 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{4λ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{16λ^{2}} &= 36 \\\\\n\\frac{9}{16λ^{2}} &= 36 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= \\frac{1}{64} \\\\\nλ &= ± \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\, y$ and $z$. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 4 \\qquad y = \\pm 4 \\qquad z= \\pm 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n$(-4, -4, -1), \\qquad (4, 4, 1)$.\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, (-4, -4, -1) \\,$ | $\\, (4, 4, 1) \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, f(x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, -9 \\,$ | $\\, 9 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4, 4, 1)$, and its maximum value is $9$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186121430 17592186121431 17592186121432 17592186121433 17592186121434] "immutable_fields" [[17592186121429 :answer-field/correct] [17592186121429 :answer-field/id] [17592186121429 :answer-field/key] [17592186121429 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
